{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":"# The Intermediate Value Theorem Fails in $\\mathbb{Q}$\n\n## Statement of the I"}}
{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":"VT\n\nIn $\\mathbb{R}$: if $f : [a,b] \\to \\mathbb{R}$ is continuous and $y$ lies strictly between"}}
{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":" $f(a)$ and $f(b)$, then there exists $c \\in (a,b)$ such that $f(c) = y$.\n\nWe show that"}}
{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":" the analogous statement is **false** in $\\mathbb{Q}$.\n\n---\n\n## A Counterexample\n\nDefine"}}
{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":"\n$$f : [0,2] \\cap \\mathbb{Q} \\longrightarrow \\mathbb{Q}, \\qquad f(x) = x^2 - 2."}}
{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":"$$\n\n**1. $f$ is continuous.** Being a polynomial, $f$ is continuous in the metric in"}}
{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":"herited from $\\mathbb{R}$ \u2014 for every rational $x_0$ and"}}
{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":" every $\\varepsilon > 0$, one finds $\\delta > 0$ such that $|x - x_0| < \\delta \\"}}
{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":"Rightarrow |f(x) - f(x_0)| < \\varepsilon$.\n\n**2. $f$ changes sign.**\n$"}}
{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":"$f(0) = -2 < 0 < 2 = f(2).$$\nThe value $y = 0$"}}
{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":" lies strictly between $f(0)$ and $f(2)$.\n\n**3. No rational satisfies $f(c) = 0$.** This would"}}
{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":" require $c^2 = 2$, i.e. $c = \\sqrt{2}$.\n\n> **Lemma.** $\\sq"}}
{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":"rt{2} \\notin \\mathbb{Q}$.\n>\n> Suppose $\\sqrt{2} = p\/q$ with $\\"}}
{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":"gcd(p,q) = 1$. Then $p^2 = 2q^2$, so $p$ is even; write $p = 2k$. Then $4"}}
{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":"k^2 = 2q^2$, i.e. $q^2 = 2k^2$, so $q$ is also even. This contradicts $\\gcd(p,q)"}}
{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":"=1$. $\\blacksquare$\n\nSo there is **no** $c \\in [0,2] \\cap \\"}}
{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":"mathbb{Q}$ with $f(c) = 0$, although such a $c$ would be"}}
{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":" required by the IVT.\n\n---\n\n## A Sharper Illustration\n\nThe failure is even"}}
{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":" more striking with\n$$g : \\mathbb{Q} \\to \\mathbb{Q}, \\qquad g(x) = \\begin{c"}}
{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":"ases} -1 & \\text{if } x^2 < 2, \\\\[2pt] +1 & \\text{if } x^2 > 2."}}
{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":" \\end{cases}$$\n\n- $g$ is **continuous on $\\mathbb{Q}$**: for every rational $x_0"}}
{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":"$, we have $x_0^2 \\neq 2$, so $g$ is locally constant near $x_0$.\n- $g("}}
{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":"0) = -1$ and $g(2) = +1$.\n- $g$ never takes the value $0$, even though $-1 < 0 < 1$."}}
{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":"\n\nA continuous rational-valued function on $\\mathbb{Q}$ can therefore *jump* over an"}}
{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":" entire range of values.\n\n---\n\n## Why This Happens\n\nThe IVT is logically equivalent ("}}
{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":"over the ordered field axioms) to the **completeness** of $\\mathbb{R}$"}}
{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":" \u2014 every nonempty subset bounded above has a least upper bound.\n\nThe set $\\{"}}
{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":"x \\in \\mathbb{Q} : x^2 < 2\\}$ is bounded above in $\\mathbb{Q}$ but has"}}
{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":" **no rational supremum**: the natural candidate $\\sqrt{2}$ does"}}
{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":" not exist in $\\mathbb{Q}$. The \"hole\" at $\\sqrt{2}$ is precisely the"}}
{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":" gap through which $f(x)="}}
{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":"x^2-2$ slips from negative to positive without ever vanishing.\n\n$$\\boxed{\\text"}}
{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":"{IVT } \\Longleftrightarrow \\text{completeness of the underlying ordered field.}}$$"}}
{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":"\n\nSince $\\mathbb{Q}$ is not complete, the IVT cannot hold there."}}
