{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":"# The Set"}}
{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":"up for Motivic Galois Theory\n\nThe problem of motivic Galois theory is, at its heart, the d"}}
{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":"ream of a **single Galois-theoretic structure** that governs the cohomology of algebraic varieties \u2014"}}
{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":" generalizing classical Galois theory of number fields and unifying the disparate cohomology theories"}}
{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":" that arise in algebraic geometry. To appreciate the problem, we need to wal"}}
{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":"k through several layers.\n\n## 1. Classical Galois theory as a starting point\n\nRecall that for a"}}
{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":" Galois extension $L\/K$, the group $\\mathrm{Gal}(L\/K)$ encodes the symmetries among algebraic relations. Most"}}
{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":" importantly:\n\n$$G_\\mathbb{Q} := \\mathrm{Gal}(\\over"}}
{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":"line{\\mathbb{Q}}\/\\mathbb{Q})$$\n\nis the absolute Galois group of $\\mathbb{Q}$ \u2014 a"}}
{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":" profinite object whose representations are the central objects of mod"}}
{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":"ern number theory.\n\nThe first key idea is that **a group"}}
{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":" can be recovered from its category of representations**. This is Tannaka\u2013Krein duality.\n\n## 2. The"}}
{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":" Tannakian formalism\n\nA *neutral Tannakian category* over a field $k$ is, roughly, a $k"}}
{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":"$-linear abelian tensor category $\\mathcal{T}$ equipped with a fiber functor $\\omega: \\mathcal{"}}
{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":"T} \\to \\mathrm{Vec}_k$ that is exact and tensor-preserving. The"}}
{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":" fundamental theorem says:\n\n$$\\mathcal{T} \\simeq \\mathrm{"}}
{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":"Rep}_k(G), \\qquad G = \\underline{\\mathrm{Aut}}^\\ot"}}
{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":"imes(\\omega).$$\n\nThat is, $G$ \u2014 an affine group scheme over"}}
{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":" $k$ \u2014 is reconstructed as the automorphisms of the fiber functor.\n\nThis is powerful because **"}}
{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":"we may have a category with the right formal structure but no group"}}
{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":" in sight**; the formalism then *defines* the group for us.\n\n## 3."}}
{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":" Cohomology of algebraic varieties\n\nFor a smooth projective variety $X$ over $\\mathbb{Q}$, several cohomology theories co"}}
{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":"exist:\n\n- **Betti**: $H^*_B(X(\\mathbb{C}), \\mathbb{Q})$ \u2014 sing"}}
{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":"ular cohomology of complex points.\n- **Algebraic de Rham**: $H^*_{\\"}}
{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":"mathrm{dR}}(X\/\\mathbb{Q})$ \u2014 hypercohomology of $\\Omega^\\"}}
{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":"bullet_{X\/\\mathbb{Q}}$.\n- **$\\ell$-adic \u00E9tale**: $H^*_"}}
{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":"\\mathrm{\\acute{e}t}(X_{\\overline{\\mathbb{Q}}}, \\mathbb{Q}_"}}
{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":"\\ell)$ \u2014 carries a $G_\\mathbb{Q}$-action.\n\nThese are lin"}}
{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":"ked by **comparison isomorphisms**, e.g.\n\n$$H^*_B(X(\\mathbb{C}), \\mathb"}}
{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":"b{Q}) \\otimes_\\mathbb{Q} \\mathbb{C} \\;\\xrightarrow{\\sim}\\; H^*_{\\mathrm{d"}}
{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":"R}}(X\/\\mathbb{Q}) \\otimes_\\mathbb{Q} \\mathbb{C}.$$\n\nEach theory loo"}}
{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":"ks \"different\" but produces the same Betti numbers, the same Hodge numbers ("}}
{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":"roughly), the same $L$-functions. Why?\n\n## 4. Periods"}}
{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":": where the action is\n\nThe Betti\u2013de Rham com"}}
{"type":"ping"}
{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":"parison, written in $\\mathbb{Q}$-rational b"}}
{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":"ases on each side, yields a matrix of complex numbers: the **periods** of $X$.\n\nKey examples:\n- $"}}
{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":"X = \\mathbb{G}_m$: the period $\\int_{|z"}}
{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":"|=1} \\frac{dz}{z} = 2\\pi i$.\n- $X$ an elliptic curve: the periods $"}}
{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":"\\omega_1, \\omega_2$ of an invariant differential.\n- $X = \\mathbb{P}^1 "}}
{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":"\\setminus \\{0,1,\\infty\\}$: iterated integrals give $\\zeta(n)$ and mult"}}
{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":"iple zeta values.\n\nThese numbers occupy a fascinating middle ground between"}}
{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":" algebraic and transcendental, and one quickly suspects there is a *"}}
{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":"symmetry group* governing the relations among them.\n\n## 5. Gr"}}
{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":"othendieck's vision: motives\n\nGrothendieck proposed there should be a **"}}
{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":"universal cohomology theory** through which all others factor. Concretely:\n\n- A category"}}
{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":" $\\mathcal{M}(k)$ of motives over $k$.\n- A functor $h: \\mathr"}}
{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":"m{Var}(k) \\to \\mathcal{M}(k)$.\n- Each cohomology theory $H^*_?"}}
{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":"$ factors as a *realization functor* $\\mathc"}}
{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":"al{M}(k) \\to (\\text{stuff})$.\n\nFor pure motives (smooth projective $X$), one"}}
{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":" builds objects out of pairs $(X, p"}}
{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":")$ where $p$ is an idempotent algebraic correspondence, and morphisms are corre"}}
{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":"spondences modulo a chosen equivalence (rational, homological, numerical). The cho"}}
{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":"ice matters: only with the **standard conjectures** does one obtain an abelian,"}}
{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":" semisimple, neutral Tannakian category.\n\nFor mixed motives (general $X$), one wants a much"}}
{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":" richer category \u2014 the existence of an abelian category $\\mathrm{M"}}
{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":"M}(k)$ remains conjectural, though Voevodsky constructed a be"}}
{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":"autiful triangulated version $\\mathrm{DM}(k)$.\n\n## 6. The motivic Galois group\n\nSuppose we have $"}}
{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":"\\mathcal{M}(k)$ as a neutral Tannakian category, with the Betti realization $\\om"}}
{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":"ega_B$ as fiber functor. Then we obtain:\n\n$$G_{"}}
{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":"\\mathrm{mot}}(k) := \\underline{\\mathrm{Aut}}^\\otimes(\\omega_B),$$\n\na p"}}
{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":"ro-algebraic group over $\\mathbb{Q}$ \u2014 the **motivic Galois group**"}}
{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":".\n\nExpected features:\n- Each motive $M$ gives a representation $G_{\\mathrm{"}}
{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":"mot}} \\to \\mathrm{GL}(\\omega_B(M))$.\n- The classical Galois group $G_"}}
{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":"\\mathbb{Q}$ should appear (e.g., as $\\pi_0$ of $"}}
{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":"G_{\\mathrm{mot}}$, or related quotients).\n- For the subcategory of mixed"}}
{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":" Tate motives over $\\mathbb{Z}$, $G_{\\mathrm{mot}}$ is f"}}
{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":"airly explicit and intimately tied to multiple zeta values ("}}
{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":"work of Deligne, Goncharov, Brown).\n\n## 7. The problems, s"}}
{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":"harply stated\n\nNow we can articulate what \"the"}}
{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":" problem of motivic Galois theory\" really is:\n\n**(a) Constructing the category.** Build"}}
{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":" $\\mathcal{M}(k)$ \u2014 pure or mixed \u2014 as an abelian,"}}
{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":" neutral Tannakian category. For pure motives, this requires the standard conjectures (K\u00FCnneth, Hod"}}
{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":"ge type, etc.). For mixed motives, even formulating the correct $t"}}
{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":"$-structure on $\\mathrm{DM}(k)$ is a major open problem.\n\n**(b) The"}}
{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":" period conjecture.** Grothendieck's conjecture asserts that for a motive $M$ over $\\mathbb{Q}"}}
{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":"$, the transcendence degree of the field generated by its periods equals $"}}
{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":"\\dim G_{\\mathrm{mot}}(M)$. This is an *enormous* generalization of Lindemann\u2013Weierstrass and"}}
{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":" remains wide open even in seemingly basic cases \u2014 e.g., the"}}
{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":" algebraic independence of $\\pi$ and $\\zeta(3)$."}}
{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":"\n\n**(c) Computing $G_{\\mathrm{mot}}$.** Even granting the fram"}}
{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":"ework, identifying the motivic Galois group of specific motives or sub-T"}}
{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":"annakian categories is a deep question. Successes (Tate motives, certain hyperg"}}
{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":"eometric motives) have driven enormous activity at the interface of number"}}
{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":" theory, mathematical physics (Feynman integrals), and combinatorics (Hopf algebras of M"}}
{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":"ZVs).\n\n**(d) Galois action and arithmetic.** How does $"}}
{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":"G_\\mathbb{Q}$ sit inside $G_{\\mathrm{mot}}$? The M"}}
{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":"umford\u2013Tate conjecture, the Tate conjecture, and the Hodge conjecture are"}}
{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":" all shadows of how restrictive the motivic Galois group should be \u2014"}}
{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":" predictions about which $\\ell$-adic or Hodge-theoretic structures act"}}
{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":"ually come from algebraic geometry.\n\n---\n\nThe unifying picture:"}}
{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":" **classical Galois theory is the Galois theory of zero-dimensional varieties ("}}
{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":"number fields). Motivic Galois theory is what you get when you allow varieties of"}}
{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":" arbitrary dimension and try to understand the symmetries among all their cohomological invariants sim"}}
{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":"ultaneously.** The \"problem\" is that this beautiful picture is, even today, only partially const"}}
{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":"ructed \u2014 and the parts that work shed light on some of the deepest quest"}}
{"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":"ions in mathematics."}}
