                       Foundations of Motivic Galois Theory:
                            An Interim Working Paper
     Toward a Tannakian Framework Unifying Cohomological Symmetries of Algebraic Varieties

                         Internal Research Note · Working Draft v0.1 · Circulated for comment


                                                         Abstract
          This note lays the conceptual and structural groundwork for the problem of a motivic Galois theory.
          Beginning with classical Galois theory of number fields and the Tannakian reconstruction of affine
          group schemes from their representation categories, we review the multiplicity of cohomology
          theories attached to a smooth projective variety, the comparison isomorphisms relating them, and the
          periods that quantify these comparisons. We then sketch Grothendieck's vision of a universal
          cohomology theory through which all realizations factor, the resulting category of motives, and the
          pro-algebraic group Gmot obtained as the Tannakian automorphism group of the Betti fibre functor.
          We close by formulating four open problems that, taken together, constitute the present scope of the
          subject: construction of the abelian category of mixed motives, the period conjecture, computation
          of motivic Galois groups in concrete cases, and the position of the absolute Galois group inside its
          motivic counterpart. The note is preparatory; technical developments are deferred to subsequent
          drafts.



 1. Classical Galois Theory as a Starting Point
     For a Galois extension L/K, the group Gal(L/K) encodes the symmetries among the algebraic relations
 satisfied by elements of L over K. The central object in modern arithmetic is the absolute Galois group of the
 rationals,
                                                     GQ := Gal(Q̄/Q),

 a profinite group whose continuous representations control vast portions of number theory. A first organising
 principle, fundamental to what follows, is that a group can be recovered from its category of representations. This
 is the content of Tannaka–Krein duality, and it is the technical engine that allows us to define Galois-type groups
 even when no group is initially in sight.

 2. The Tannakian Formalism
 A neutral Tannakian category over a field k is, in essence, a k-linear abelian rigid tensor category T equipped
 with an exact, faithful, tensor-preserving fibre functor ω: T → Veck. The fundamental theorem of
 Saavedra–Deligne asserts a tensor equivalence
                                              T ≃ Repk(G), G := Aut (ω),

    where G is an affine group scheme over k, reconstructed as the tensor-automorphisms of the fibre functor.
 The slogan: specify a category with the right formal structure, and a group emerges. This is precisely the strategy
 adopted in motivic Galois theory: the category will be that of motives, the fibre functor a chosen cohomological
 realization.

 3. The Cohomology of Algebraic Varieties
 Let X be a smooth projective variety over Q. Several cohomology theories coexist:

 •    Betti cohomology: H*B(X(C), Q), the singular cohomology of the complex points.

 •    Algebraic de Rham cohomology: H*dR(X/Q), the hypercohomology of the algebraic de Rham complex
      Ω•X/Q.

