 •    ℓ-adic étale cohomology: H*ét(XQ̄, Qℓ), carrying a continuous action of GQ.

 These theories are linked by canonical comparison isomorphisms; the Betti–de Rham comparison reads
                                        H*B(X(C), Q) Q C ≃ H*dR(X/Q) Q C.

    Each realization yields the same Betti numbers, the same Hodge numbers (in the appropriate sense), and the
 same L-functions. The coherence among these distinct constructions is the principal phenomenon that motivic
 theory seeks to explain.

 4. Periods
 Choosing Q-rational bases on each side of the Betti–de Rham comparison produces a matrix of complex
 numbers, the periods of X. Representative examples:

 •    X = Gm: the period ∫|z|=1 dz/z = 2πi.

 •    X an elliptic curve: the periods ω1, ω2 of an invariant differential.

 •    X = P1 ∖ {0, 1, ∞}: iterated integrals giving ζ(n) and, more generally, multiple zeta values.

 These numbers occupy a delicate position between the algebraic and the transcendental. The empirical and
 conjectural relations among them strongly suggest the action of a hidden symmetry group — the motivic Galois
 group, to be defined below.

 5. Grothendieck's Vision: Motives
 Grothendieck proposed the existence of a universal cohomology theory through which all Weil cohomologies
 factor. Schematically, one seeks:

 •    a category M(k) of motives over k;

 •    a contravariant functor h: Var(k) → M(k);

 •    for each cohomology theory H*?, a realization functor M(k) → (target category) through which H*? factors.

 For pure motives (X smooth projective), one builds objects from pairs (X, p) with p an idempotent algebraic
 correspondence; morphisms are correspondences modulo a chosen equivalence relation (rational, homological,
 numerical). The choice is consequential: only under the standard conjectures does one obtain an abelian,
 semisimple, neutral Tannakian category. For mixed motives (general X) the desired abelian category MM(k)
 remains conjectural, although Voevodsky's triangulated category DM(k) provides a robust working substitute.

 6. The Motivic Galois Group
 Granting M(k) as a neutral Tannakian category and taking the Betti realization ωB as fibre functor, the
 Tannakian formalism produces
                                                   Gmot(k) := Aut (ωB),

 a pro-algebraic group over Q — the motivic Galois group. Its expected features include:

 •    every motive M yielding a representation Gmot → GL(ωB(M));

 •    the classical Galois group GQ appearing as a quotient or component (e.g. as π0 of Gmot);

 •    for the subcategory of mixed Tate motives over Z, an explicit description intimately linked to multiple zeta
      values (Deligne, Goncharov, Brown).

